Let $F = (f,g) : \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^2 \to \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^2$ be given in \eqref{f1}. 
The phase portrait of the vector field $\mathcal{Y}_{\mathcal{X}_{\textnormal{r}}}$ in the Poincaré disc is shown in Figure~\ref{example111}.

\label{chartU2}
The Poincar\'e compactification of the vector field $\nabla H^{\perp}$ in a neighborhood of the origin of the chart $U_2$ is constituted of two degenerate hyperbolic sectors as shown in Figure~\ref{sing2}. 
\begin{figure}[htpb]
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[scale=.28]{sing2.eps}
\end{center}
\caption{The origin of the chart $U_2$.}
\label{sing2}
\end{figure}

By homogeneity, it is not difficult we see that the infinite singular points of the vector field \ref{tre} is given by the zeros of the real linear factors of the expression
\begin{equation}\label{inf1}
(m-n) f^{n} g^{m},
\end{equation}
where $f^{n}$ and $g^{m}$ are the higher homogeneous parts, of degree $n$ and $m$, of $f$ and $g$, respectively. 
In our case we have $f^{9}=4x^6y^3$ and $g^{15}=-16x^{10}y^5$, so the result follows.

Note that $S_{15} \rightarrow S_{16}, S_{17}, S_{20}, S_{21}$ or the origin. 
Clearly $S_{15} \nrightarrow S_{16}$ and $S_{15} \nrightarrow S_{17}$. 
\begin{itemize}
\item Assume $S_{15} \rightarrow S_{20}$.
\end{itemize}
Then $S_{16} \rightarrow S_{18}$ or $S_{19}$. 
In the first possibility, it follows that $S_{19}$ has empty $\alpha$-limit set. 
In the second one, we will have the connection of hyperbolic sectors and so the existence of a finite singular point there, a contradiction. 

\begin{itemize}
\item Assume $S_{15} \rightarrow S_{21}$.
\end{itemize}
The same reasons apply to leads to a contradiction. 

Therefore, the lemma follows.

\label{curvas}
The fiber $f^{-1}(0)$ has five connected components, while the fiber $g^{-1}(0)$ has seven connected components. 
Their topological configurations and relative ordering in the real plane are shown in Figure \ref{f0g0}.

\label{raizesfzero}
The roots $R_{f}^1(x)$ and $R_g^1(x)$ tend to $0$ as $x\to 0^+$. 
The roots $R_{f}^2(x)$, $R_{f}^3(x)$, $R_{g}^2(x)$, and $R_{g}^3(x)$ tend to $-\infty$ as $x\to 0^+$.

\label{xy}
Over the line $x = 0$, the vector field \ref{P44} is constant and equal to $\left(-1, 1\right)$. 
Over the line $y=0$ it is given by $\left( -1 - 5 x^2 - 12 x^4, 1 + 12 x^2 \right)$; in particular, its first coordinate is negative and its second coordinate is positive.

\label{importante}
$$
\begin{aligned}
JF \mathcal{Y}_{\mathcal{X}_{\textnormal{a}}} (0, y) & =  (-y,0), \\
JF \mathcal{Y}_{\mathcal{X}_{\textnormal{a}}} (x, 0) & =  (-4x^3(75+ \sum_{i=2}^{24} a_i x^{2i}+ c x^{52}),\; 16x(16+\sum_{k=2}^{24} a_k x^{2k} + d x^{52})), 
\end{aligned}
$$
where $c$, $d$, $a_i$, and $a_k$ are positive integers for all $i$ and $k$.

\label{ra}
The separatrices of the hyperbolic sectors $S_{13}P_1S_{12}$, $S_{21}P_1S_{20}$, $S_9 P_3 S_{10}$ and $S_1 P_3 S_2$ belong to $H^{-1}(40)$. 
The separatrices of the hyperbolic sector $S_{14}P_1S_{15}$ and $S_8 P_3 S_7$, as well as the orbits $S_{22}$ and $S_{11}$, belong to $H^{-1}(\alpha)$.
